OSI model:  OSI == opensystem internconnection model!



the 7 layres of networking 


-----------------------------
Layer 1: Physical (Bottom)
Layer 2: Data Link
Layer 3: Network
Layer 4: Transport
Layer 5: Session
Layer 6: Presentation
Layer 7: Application (Top)
-----------------------------


==>     layer 1:    the physical layer!

this is the lowest level, responsible for transmitting raw bits 0, 1 over physical meduim

it has no understanding of packets heaaders, IP addesses, Mac addresse or the content of the data!

this layer is made of:

copper cables
wifi
fiber cables
hardware devices: ethernet hubs, network interface cards, modems and so on...

--> this layer doesnt know where the data is going
--> all it can see if the a s tream of data
--> it just cables and that is!!!

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

==>     level 2: the data link layer!

layer 1: just cables to move raw bits!

layer 2 takes those bits, structure them into discrete chunks called frames, and adds physical mac addresses to make sure that the data reach the targeted device without needing to rout over the global internet

==> the switch operates primarly at level 2, its job essentially is:
    "i have a frame for MAC x:x:x:, which port should i send it!"

==> in this layer, we atake the raw data from the physical layer and organize it into frames
==> look below for frames!

layer 2 is split into 2 parts:
    - media access address: MAC


takes the bits from level 1, and addds actual structure to it!
it organize the 1s and 0s into frames

frames:
    wrapping our data with imporatnt addressing information!
    so it knows where to go on the local network 
    this is where we add the mac addresses
    the data link layer uses this mac address ot make sure that our data reaches it targeted
    destination!!

how does framing work?
    1- we take the data, 01010010101010101001010101
    2- at the front we add the sender and recevier mac_address: x:x:x:x | x:x:x:x  0101001_data_010
    3- at the back, we add error detection bits, called the frame check sequence!
    4- frame check seuqnce verfiy that the data wasnt corrupoted, if it was, the drame is thrown out

how is this layer important?
    in the old days, we had hub instead of switches
    hub used to send the frame to all the devices that are connected to it
    so switch, in this layer also handles the data/frames transmitting in the LAN
    hence the name, media access control
    --> the data link layer has protocols like: CSMA/CD
    this protocol detcts these clliions and tell devices to wait random amount of time before trying again


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

==>     level 3: the network layer!

it scrucial for routing data across different networks!

if you wanna send data accross the internet to server in another country, MAC addresses are useless
thats where the network layer comes in

this layer is responsible for logical addrresing and routing
this is where the IP addresses come into play!

mac == LAN
IP address == lets you communcatine with any this layer is responsible for logical addrresing and routing
this is where the IP addresses come into play!

mac == LAN
IP address == lets you communcatine with any device anywhere on the internet


layer 2 == communication between LAN
layer 3 == handles communcation between different networks

every device has an IP address!
the network layer, takes your data and wrapts it in a packet!

this packet includes:
    your pc IP address
    the destination IP address -> the server its trying to reach
    ==> then figures out the best path to get that packet from your LAN to the destination network

--> this is where routers come in
routers sit between networks and make intelligenet decision s about where to send packets

when a packet reaches a router, the routers check which path leads to towards that destination
and forwards the packet towards the next router
this process repeats across multiple routers untilll the packet reaches its destination

--> this is where we need to learn about subnet masks!
subnet masks determines which part of the ip address determines the network
and which part of the specific device
--> this is also where you should learn how routers use protocols like RIP, OSPF, and BGP
these protocols are used by routers to get the best routing path and build those routing tables

they can explain why your data might take a different path across the internet depending on network
congestion or link failure

==> another thing about the networking layer is that it handles data fragmentation

if a packet is too large to be sent in one go
it breaks down the packet into small parts, and get reessemble at the destination end


